---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "serious mass protein powder",
      "optimum nutrition serious mass protein powder",
      "optimum nutrition serious mass weight gainer",
      "serious mass protein powder review"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Weight Gain Picks",
    link: "/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Brand Review"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Serious Mass Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Optimum Nutrition Serious Mass is one of the biggest-calorie gainers on the shelf. Here is what is in a full 340 g serving, what it really costs, and who should take half.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#short-answer">Short Answer</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Products</a>
      <a href="#whats-in-it">What Is in It</a>
      <a href="#serving-size">Serving Size and Half Doses</a>
      <a href="#third-party-tested">Third-Party Testing</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">Taste, Mixing and Digestion</a>
      <a href="#vs-pro-gainer">Serious Mass vs Pro Gainer</a>
      <a href="#is-it-healthy">Sugar and Health</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Serious Mass is a cheap, effective way to drink about 1,250 to 1,260 calories, but only 16% of those calories come from protein. A full 340 g serving gives 50 g of protein, 253 g of carbohydrate and 5 g of creatine, per ON's label. Your appetite, blood sugar and budget per gram of protein decide whether it suits you. Below: the label, serving advice, testing, cost and better-fitting alternatives.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Serious Mass Protein Powder: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Serious Mass is worth buying if you genuinely struggle to eat enough, and a poor choice if you mainly want more protein. A full serving delivers about 1,250 to 1,260 calories, 50 g of protein, 253 g of carbohydrate and 5 g of creatine monohydrate, per Optimum Nutrition's label at the time of writing, so it is a calorie product first and a protein product a distant second.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Buy it if</strong> you are underweight or a true hard gainer, you train hard, you can blend it with milk, and you are comfortable starting at half a serving. <strong>Skip it if</strong> you have diabetes or prediabetes, you want lean gains, you are sensitive to lactose, or you just want a protein shake. For those readers, <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-weight-gain/">our weight gain protein powder picks</a> include higher-protein, lower-sugar options.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Serious Mass by the Numbers (per ON label)</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">1,260</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Calories per 340 g serving (Vanilla)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">50 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein, all from whey concentrate</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">253 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Carbohydrate, mostly maltodextrin</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Creatine monohydrate per serving</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Serious Mass Sizes and the Gainers Worth Comparing
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two Serious Mass sizes, ON's own higher-protein Pro Gainer, a cheaper big gainer and a cleaner three-ingredient option. Prices are what we saw on Amazon on 18 November 2026 and change often; cost per 25 g of protein is worked out from each label's servings and protein per serving.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="whats-in-it" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is in Optimum Nutrition Serious Mass?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Optimum Nutrition Serious Mass is mostly maltodextrin plus whey protein concentrate. The ingredient list on the current Vanilla label reads: maltodextrin, whey protein concentrate, natural and artificial flavor, sunflower and/or soy lecithin, acesulfame potassium and sucralose, with a separate vitamin and mineral blend and 5 g of creatine monohydrate in the supplement facts. The allergen statement says it contains milk and soy.
          </p>

          <p>
            Per the Vanilla label at the time of writing, one 340 g serving gives 1,260 calories, 5 g of total fat (3 g saturated), 200 mg of cholesterol, 253 g of total carbohydrate, 30 g of total sugars including 8 g of added sugars, 50 g of protein, 680 mg of sodium and 940 mg of potassium. ON's product page gives a range of 1,250 to 1,260 calories and 250 to 253 g of carbohydrate across its five flavors, so your flavor may be a few calories either side.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Per 340 g serving (Vanilla label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Amount</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Calories</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,260</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Protein</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50 g (whey protein concentrate)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total carbohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">253 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total sugars / added sugars</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">30 g / 8 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Total fat / saturated fat</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g / 3 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sodium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">680 mg</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Creatine monohydrate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5 g</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sweeteners</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame potassium</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The protein numbers are respectable on their own. ON says all 50 g come from whey protein concentrate and that a serving provides 11 g of BCAAs, 24 g of essential amino acids and 8 g of naturally occurring glutamine. That last figure matters because older Serious Mass tubs advertised added glutamine; on the current page, ON describes the glutamine as naturally occurring in the whey, not an extra ingredient. Creatine, by contrast, is a genuine addition: 5 g of creatine monohydrate per full serving is the standard daily dose used in research.
          </p>

          <p>
            Calories are where the formula gets lopsided. 50 g of protein supplies about 200 of the 1,260 calories, roughly 16%, while 253 g of carbohydrate supplies about 1,000, or roughly 80%. Fat is nearly absent. That is a deliberate design: maltodextrin is cheap, easy to digest in liquid form and adds calories without making the shake greasy. It also explains why Serious Mass costs little per calorie but a lot per gram of protein, which we work out below.
          </p>

          <p>
            The vitamin and mineral blend covers 19 nutrients per ON, including 54% of the daily value for calcium, 32% for iron and 200% for biotin in a full serving. That is helpful if your diet is poor, but if you already take a multivitamin you are doubling up on several nutrients, so check the overlap before stacking the two.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="serving-size" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. How Many Scoops Is a Serving of Serious Mass, and Should You Take Half?
          </h2>

          <p>
            A serving of Serious Mass is 340 g, which ON describes as about two heaping scoops, mixed into 24 fl oz of cold water, milk or another drink and blended for 30 to 45 seconds. That is a very large shake. In practice it is closer to a meal replacement in volume than a protein shake, and most people cannot drink it in one sitting without discomfort at first.
          </p>

          <p>
            ON itself recommends easing in. The suggested-use text on its product page says new users may find it beneficial to begin with half a serving for the first week and then gradually increase to a full serving. A half serving, one heaping scoop of about 170 g, gives roughly 630 calories, 25 g of protein, 126 g of carbohydrate and 2.5 g of creatine, which is still a substantial addition to most diets.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Water or milk?</h3>

          <p>
            The label figures are for the powder alone. Mixing a full serving with 24 fl oz of whole milk adds roughly 450 calories and about 24 g of protein, for a shake of around 1,700 calories and about 74 g of protein. That is a useful lever: whole milk raises the protein share of the shake and improves texture, while water keeps it lighter. Buyers who struggle with fullness often blend with water and split the shake across the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Splitting is the most practical tip for most people. Two half servings, one mid-morning and one after training, add the same 1,250 or so calories with far less stomach strain than one giant shake. It also spreads the 5 g of creatine across the day, which makes no real difference to how creatine works, since it is the daily total that counts. If you already take creatine, our <a href="/creatine-vs-protein-powder/">creatine vs protein powder guide</a> explains why you do not need a second scoop on top.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not assume more is better. A full serving every day adds about 8,800 calories a week before food. For a lean hard gainer that may be exactly the point; for anyone who already eats reasonably well, it can mean fat gain rather than muscle. A sensible target is a weight gain of around 0.25 to 0.5 kg a week, adjusting the dose up or down when the scale stalls or climbs too fast.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="third-party-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Is Serious Mass Third-Party Tested?
          </h2>

          <p>
            We could not find a product-level third-party certification for Serious Mass. The Serious Mass label and US product page we checked do not show an Informed Choice, Informed Sport or NSF Certified for Sport mark, and ON does not claim one for this product on the page. That is not proof the product is untested, only that a certification is not advertised.
          </p>

          <p>
            What ON does say, on its Our Quality page, is that its global production facilities hold certifications from LGC Informed Choice, NSF, ISO and BRCGS, and that it runs over 100,000 quality and food safety checks a year at its owned facilities. Facility certification and in-house testing are meaningful for quality control, but they are not the same as batch-level banned-substance testing of a specific product by an independent lab.
          </p>

          <p>
            The contrast with ON's own Pro Gainer is useful. The current Pro Gainer label carries a Banned Substance Tested mark on the front panel, so if you compete in a drug-tested sport and want an ON gainer, Pro Gainer is the one with a visible claim. You should still confirm the specific product and batch in the certifier's database before relying on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            If certification is a must, the cleanest swap on our list is Naked Mass, which Naked Nutrition says is NSF certified. ON's Gold Standard whey line also promotes Informed Choice testing, which our <a href="/optimum-nutrition-protein-powder-review/">Optimum Nutrition protein powder review</a> covers in detail. For a recreational lifter who is not tested, the lack of a mark on Serious Mass is a minor point; for a competitive athlete, it should be a deal-breaker until ON publishes one.
