          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. What Do Buyers Say About Serious Mass Taste, Mixing and Digestion?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buyer reviews of Serious Mass are broadly positive on taste and results, and mixed on digestion. We have not tasted it ourselves; the themes below come from public buyer reviews and independent write-ups. Across retailers and review sites, the most common positive comment is simply that it helped people gain weight when food alone had not, and many reviewers say they have reordered the product for years.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Taste and best flavor</h3>

          <p>
            Chocolate is the flavor buyers praise most often, with Chocolate Peanut Butter also popular. Vanilla gets mixed reports: some buyers like it, while others describe a typical artificial vanilla taste that works better blended with fruit. Banana and Strawberry draw the most divided reviews. A common tip is to blend Vanilla with a banana and peanut butter, which also adds calories and some fat the formula lacks.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Mixing</h3>

          <p>
            Mixing is where buyers repeatedly say to use a blender. With 340 g of powder in 24 fl oz of liquid, a shaker bottle often leaves clumps, while a blender produces a smooth shake. ON's own directions call for 30 to 45 seconds in a blender, which tells you something. Buyers also note that the scoop is easy to lose in a 12 lb bag, so fishing it out before you start is a small but common complaint.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Digestion and bloating</h3>

          <p>
            Bloating and gas are the most frequent complaints, especially from people who start at the full two-scoop serving. Some reviewers report no stomach trouble at all; others report heavy bloating and, occasionally, skin breakouts. Two causes are likely: the sheer volume of carbohydrate in one sitting and the lactose in whey protein concentrate. Starting at half a serving, splitting doses and blending with water or lactose-free milk are the fixes buyers mention most.
          </p>
